The C++ library already has a function for this task. `lower_bound` returns an iterator pointing to the first element that is not smaller than `x`. Converting the iterator difference to an integer gives the required index.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
public:
    int lowerBound(vector<int> &nums, int x){
        return static_cast<int>(lower_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), x) - nums.begin());
    }
};
```

The input array is not changed. If `x` is larger than all values, the iterator is `nums.end()`, so the returned index is `nums.size()`.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

Python's `bisect_left` directly finds the first position whose value is at least `x`. It follows the same sorted-array rule as binary search, but keeps the search code in the standard library.

```python
from bisect import bisect_left

class Solution:
    def lowerBound(self, nums, x):
        return bisect_left(nums, x)
```

A dry run for `nums = [1, 2, 2, 5]` and `x = 2` returns `1`, not `2`, because the leftmost matching position is wanted. The list stays unchanged. This depends on `nums` being sorted.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

The search keeps a half-open range `[left, right)`. When `nums[mid]` is at least `x`, `mid` can be the answer, so the right side moves to `mid`. Otherwise, the answer must be after `mid`.

A small dry run with `[2, 4, 6, 8]` and `x = 5` ends with `left = 2`, which is the position before `6`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int lowerBound(int[] nums, int x) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.length;

        while (left < right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (nums[mid] >= x) {
                right = mid;
            } else {
                left = mid + 1;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }
}
```

For an empty array, `left` starts at `0` and that same value is returned. The array must be sorted for the binary search to work.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

A simple forward scan works because the array is already sorted. The first value that is greater than or equal to `x` is the answer. If every value is smaller, the loop ends and returns `len(nums)`.

For example, with `nums = [1, 3, 3, 7]` and `x = 3`, the scan stops at index `1`.

```python
class Solution:
    def lowerBound(self, nums, x):
        for index, value in enumerate(nums):
            if value >= x:
                return index
        return len(nums)
```

This also handles an empty list, where the result is `0`. The trade-off is that it may check every element.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

This version first grows the right boundary by powers of two. That quickly finds a part of the array where the answer can be, then a normal binary search finishes the job. It is useful when the answer is near the beginning, although the array length is still known here.

```go
func lowerBound(nums []int, x int) int {
	if len(nums) == 0 {
		return 0
	}

	right := 1
	for right < len(nums) && nums[right-1] < x {
		right *= 2
	}
	if right > len(nums) {
		right = len(nums)
	}

	left := right / 2
	for left < right {
		mid := left + (right-left)/2
		if nums[mid] >= x {
			right = mid
		} else {
			left = mid + 1
		}
	}
	return left
}
```

The first loop checks the last item before the current boundary, so no index outside the slice is read. For `[1, 4, 9, 12]` and `x = 10`, the boundary grows until the final search returns index `3`.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

